---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import guide from '../../data/sim/guide.json';
import data from '../../data/sim/vessel.json';
import adaptData from '../../data/sim/adapt.json';
import { refById } from '../../data/references';
import type { GuideIndex, ReportCell, TheoryIndex } from '../../sim/guide/data';
import { envelopeChart } from '../../sim/guide/envelope';
import { switchChart, type Switch } from '../../sim/guide/switch';
import { calibrate, type Params } from '../../sim/vessel';
import type { AdaptStudy } from '../../sim/figures';

// The preprint. Every number comes from the runs the site shows (src/data/sim/); nothing is typed in by hand.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const repo = 'https://github.com/ejhong/knots';
const g = guide as unknown as GuideIndex;
const byId = (id: string) => g.theories.find((t) => t.id === id)!;
const trait = (th: TheoryIndex, key: string) => th.traits.find((x) => x.id === key);
const cell = (id: string, key: string) => trait(byId(id), key)?.cell ?? '—';
const text = (id: string, key: string) => cited(trait(byId(id), key)?.text ?? '');
const p = data.params.values as unknown as Params;
const s = calibrate(p);
const col = data.checks.collar;
const cool = data.checks.cooling;
const ad = adaptData as unknown as AdaptStudy;
const setAt = (rows: { mult: number; hours: number | null }[], m: number) => rows.find((row) => Math.abs(row.mult - m) < 1e-9)?.hours ?? NaN;
const setHealthy = setAt(ad.healthy.set_h, ad.stories.hold_mult);
const f2 = (x: number) => x.toFixed(2);
const K = g.run.settings;
const rep = (rid: string, id: string) => {
  const c = g.reports?.find((r) => r.id === rid)?.theories[id] as ReportCell | undefined;
  return c && c.n ? `${c.k} of ${c.n}` : '—';
};

// How many numbers are measured, fitted or inferred from a quoted source, and how many are guesses: read from the tables.
const tables = import.meta.glob('../../../sim/params/*.yaml', { query: '?raw', eager: true }) as Record<string, { default: string }>;
const conf = { sourced: 0, guessed: 0 };
for (const raw of Object.values(tables).map((mod) => mod.default)) {
  for (const line of raw.split('\n')) {
    const t = line.trim();
    if (t.startsWith('confidence:')) t.endsWith('guessed') ? conf.guessed++ : conf.sourced++;
  }
}

// Citations, numbered in order of first use; the list at the end is built from the same order.
const order: string[] = [];
const cite = (...ids: string[]) => {
  const ns = ids.map((id) => {
    if (!order.includes(id)) order.push(id);
    return order.indexOf(id) + 1;
  });
  const links = ns.map((n, i) => '<a href="#ref-' + ids[i] + '">' + n + '</a>');
  return '<span class="cite">[' + links.join(', ') + ']</span>';
};
const MARK: Record<string, string> = { all: '●', some: '◐', none: '○', silent: '—', not: '·' };
// The models' own sentences cite by key, "(segal1989)": turned into this paper's numbered citations.
const KEY = new RegExp('\\(([a-z]+[0-9]{4}[a-z]?)\\)', 'g');
const cited = (t: string) => t.replace(KEY, (m: string, id: string) => (refById(id) ? cite(id) : m));
const ids = g.theories.map((t) => t.id);
---
<Doc
  title="Knots: seven mechanisms, what each would do, and a recording that would tell them apart"
  kicker="Preprint · not peer reviewed"
  current="research"
  description="A preprint: seven mechanisms for the knots people who work with the body report, each run as a model from its own physiology, what each predicts, and the recording at release that would decide."
>
  <div class="paper">
    <p class="byline">Eugene Jhong · Knots of Existence, independent · <span class="mono">28 September 2026 · draft 1 · field guide run {g.run.inputs}</span></p>
    <p class="byline mono">Correspondence and objections: <a href={`${repo}/issues`}>github.com/ejhong/knots/issues</a> · <a href={`${base}/research/preprint.pdf`}>PDF</a> · <a href={`${base}/research/pilot/`}>the pilot protocol</a></p>

    <section class="abstract">
      <h2>Abstract</h2>
      <p>People who work with the body report knots: small places that hold for months or years and let go within seconds, under a
        hand, with a slow breath or to attention, often with a tingle over the skin. No one has recorded one letting go. We set out seven
        mechanisms that could explain them: a small artery held shut where it pierces the fascia (perforators), vascular smooth muscle
        latched by a held prediction (the vascular latch), a myofascial trigger point, densified hyaluronan in the gliding layer between
        the fasciae, a sensitised nerve where it pierces the fascia, motor units latched on by persistent inward currents (a motor switch),
        and perception. Each was run as a model from its own physiology, with {conf.sourced} parameters taken from a quoted line of a
        paper and {conf.guessed} marked as guesses with ranges, through the same scenes (a stressful moment, slow breathing, attention,
        a resting hand, rolling, heat, an hour of moods) in {K} sampled settings each; what its knots did was read off the runs, not
        fitted to anyone's reports. The models part most at the moment of release: skin flow floods in (perforators), flow rises
        slowly (the latch), stiffness falls in the muscle (trigger points), the layers slide again (densification), a nerve's firing
        falls (nerves), a motor unit falls silent (the motor switch), or nothing changes in the tissue (perception). Set against
        practitioners' reports, the perforator model, extended with the sleeve of sliding tissue around each vessel, reproduces most
        of them, including release with a roller ({rep('roller', 'T1')} settings), sparks ({rep('spark', 'T1')}), stiffness
        ({rep('stiff', 'T1')}) and larger knots freeing smaller ones but not the reverse ({rep('trees', 'T1')}); releases to attention
        alone point instead to the latch ({rep('attention', 'T2')}) or the motor switch. Knots may be more than one thing. We propose a
        pilot recording at the moment of release, with laser speckle, single-motor-unit EMG and ultrasound against a pressed sham,
        whose outcome each model predicts in advance.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>1. The problem</h2>
      <p>Massage therapists, physiotherapists and contemplative practitioners describe the same thing in different words: discrete,
        tender places in the soft tissue, most often over the neck, shoulders and back, that can persist for years and yet let go
        within seconds. Accounts from contemplative practice and bodywork add detail: knots seem to answer to the breath, letting go on
        a slow out-breath; deeper ones to focused attention; a patient hand or a foam roller releases them one place at a time; a hot
        shower softens many at once; they gather where stress is held and limit movement; a release can come with a small pop or a
        tingle across a patch of skin, and another knot may move in nearby. These are impressions, not measurements, but they are
        specific enough to test.</p>
      <p>The speed is the first clue. Collagen, scar and adhesion change over days and weeks, so whatever lets go within a breath must
        be a state the body is holding, not a structure it has built. At least seven candidates have been proposed or can be
        assembled from established physiology, and they disagree about where a knot is, what holds it and what releases it. None has
        been tested against a recording of a release, because none has been made.</p>
      <p>Rather than choose among them, we ask each to show itself: to grow its own knots, from its own physiology, in the same piece of
        body under the same scenes, and to say how they would feel, respond and let go, and what an instrument would record. Where their
        knots differ in something a hand or an instrument can see, a measurement can decide.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>2. Models and methods</h2>
      <h3>2.1 One patch, the same scenes</h3>
      <p>Every model lives in the same 4 cm square of the upper back over the trapezius (skin, fat, the superficial and deep fascia,
        muscle), with stress held more toward the neck and at one place beside a spot where a hand rests and attention goes. Each places
        its own units where its anatomy puts them. The scenes are the same for all: rest, three minutes of stress, then a held level;
        thirty slow breaths (4 s in, 6 s out); the same with attention on the spot; subtle breaths with attention; a hand resting on the
        spot for a minute; a roller over a quiet place, and over the knot; five minutes of heat, as in a hot shower or a sauna; an hour of stress that wanders as moods
        do; half an hour more at the held level. Stress is one shared unit (0 rest, 1 a surge) mapped onto each theory's own inputs in a
        runner whose mapping is written out.</p>

      <h3>2.2 The seven models</h3>
      <p><strong>Perforators.</strong> A small artery with tone in its wall is a switch: the pressure inside pushes it open, its muscle
        pulls it shut, and because muscle is strongest at one length there is a band of tone over which both states are stable
        {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('burton1951', 'nichol1951')} />. Parameters come from measured wall strength, thickness and
        pressure; the breath acts through the skin's sympathetic reflex {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('mayrovitz2026')} /> and through
        movement, which cuts smooth muscle's force {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('ljung1975')} />. Vessels form trees of a parent and its
        children sharing pressure, with conducted responses along their walls {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('segal1989')} />. Each vessel
        crosses the gliding plane in a sleeve of sliding tissue {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('guimberteau2005')} /> that its flow keeps
        wet; shut, the sleeve dries, its hyaluronan crowds (viscosity rising as the third or fourth power of concentration
        {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('cowman2015')} />), and it can jam. A knot is a vessel held shut by its own wall; a shut vessel
        starves its patch, and its nerve bursts as blood returns (the spark).</p>
      <p><strong>The vascular latch.</strong> Johnson's account {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('johnson2023')} />: a held prediction
        commands a region's small arteries; the contraction clamps the region and cuts it off from awareness, so the prediction cannot
        update. Formalised as a loop between the prediction and the clamp, set from the account's two claims (durable while unattended;
        not set by ordinary stress alone); attention or touch lets the prediction update. The latch-bridge state itself economises and
        does not remember {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('rembold1991')} />.</p>
      <p><strong>Trigger points.</strong> The energy crisis {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('gerwin2004')} />: endplate activity contracts
        sarcomeres, the contraction squeezes their capillaries, and starved sarcomeres cannot relax; sustained pressure lengthens the
        contracture over the tens of seconds of pressure release; a fast release twitches.</p>
      <p><strong>Densification.</strong> The gliding layer as a thixotropic material with a critical stress
        {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('coussot2002')} />: it rebuilds a structure at rest and loses it under shear, so between two drives
        both a jammed and a fluid state hold; a jammed patch gives way slowly and then all at once. Hyaluronan alone hardly changes with
        pH in the body's range {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('gatej2005')} /> and does not gel {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('krause2001')} />,
        so a structure that jams must be more than hyaluronan.</p>
      <p><strong>Nerves.</strong> A nerve of the skin, sensitised where it pierces the fascia beside a perforator
        {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('weum2016', 'quintner1994')} />, fires when pressed or stretched after a neuritis
        {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('dilley2005', 'dilley2008')} /> and when the sympathetic drive rises, about fourteen seconds late
        {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('devor1994')} />. It has no switch: a knot is felt while its drive lasts.</p>
      <p><strong>The motor switch.</strong> Spinal motor neurons, once recruited, keep firing on a fraction of the input that recruited
        them, through persistent inward currents set by serotonin and noradrenaline {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('gorassini1998', 'heckman2008')} />;
        the metabolites of the contraction keep the loop going {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('johansson1991')} />. A knot is a latched
        unit; it unclenches at once when inhibited.</p>
      <p><strong>Perception.</strong> Central gain, arousal and attention turn ordinary input into a place felt as a knot, with a
        little hysteresis {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('woolf2011')} />. Nothing in the tissue changes.</p>

      <h3>2.3 What is felt and recorded</h3>
      <p>The same rules for all: a finger feels stiffness through skin and fat, weaker as (a/√(a² + d²))³ for a region of size a at
        depth d, above a threshold of touch; tenderness from each theory's own sensitiser; and what an instrument would record at the spot
        and at a pressed sham: skin flow by laser speckle, single motor units by surface EMG, stiffness by elastography, the layers'
        sliding by ultrasound, a nerve's firing.</p>

      <h3>2.4 Settings, scale and portraits</h3>
      <p>Each theory runs in {K} settings drawn by Sobol sampling across the ranges of its uncertain numbers. Every parameter carries
        its source and the quoted line, checked word for word against PubMed records, or is marked as a guess with a range. One shared
        rule sets each theory's scale (a stressful moment forms knots at the typical place; the held stress keeps them; the held stress
        alone does not form them); it is calibration, not a finding. Each theory's portrait is generated from the runs: a line holds in
        every setting (structural for that theory), in some (with the sampled number that best predicts where), or in none. The code
        and every number are open {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('knotsrepo')} />.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>3. Results</h2>
      <h3>3.1 What physics rules out</h3>
      <p>A liquid resists motion, not position: at its most viscous, hyaluronan around a vessel exerts a drag {Math.round(col.pressure_over_drag)}{' '}
        times weaker than the pressure holding the vessel open {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('nicholls2018', 'fede2018')} />. A patch of
        skin warms with a time constant of at least {Math.round(cool.tau_s_at_max_flow)} s even at maximal skin flow, so warming cannot
        release a knot within a breath. Warmth thins hyaluronan by only about 2% a degree {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('cowman2015')} />.
        A vessel pressed shut cannot reopen until the pressure lifts. The sympathetic drive reaches a sensitised nerve only after about
        fourteen seconds {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('devor1994')} />. And healthy small arteries hold themselves shut only over a band
        of tone ({f2(s.Aopen)}–{f2(s.Afold)} of maximal, rest {f2(s.urest)}); held shut at {ad.stories.hold_mult}× resting tone, their
        muscle adapts to the new length and the knot sets, holding at rest, after about {setHealthy.toFixed(1)} h
        {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('syyong2008', 'martinezlemus2004')} />.</p>

      <h3>3.2 The trace at release</h3>
      <p>The seven models part most sharply at the moment of release (Table 1). In every setting of each model the trace is its own; one
        recording at a release would separate them.</p>
      <table class="t">
        <caption>Table 1. What an instrument would record as a knot lets go, and what a hand would find, by model.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>At release</th><th>A finger finds</th><th>Under a resting hand</th><th>Attention alone</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {g.theories.map((th) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{th.name}</th>
              <td>{cell(th.id, 'record')}</td>
              <td>{cell(th.id, 'bump')}</td>
              <td>{cell(th.id, 'hand')}</td>
              <td>{cell(th.id, 'attention')}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>3.3 What a breath would have to do</h3>
      <p>However complex its pattern, a breath reaches a knot only through a few routes: the drive to its vessels or motor units, the
        tissue's movement, attention. For each model we found the least calming of the knot's own drive, held for a given time, that frees
        the typical knot (Figure 1). In no model does a single breath free the typical knot through drive; minutes of slow breathing do in
        some. The breath's movement, and attention where a model gives it a route, do what a single calming cannot.</p>
      <figure class="fig" set:html={envelopeChart(g.theories)} />
      <p class="figcap">Figure 1. The least calming, as a share of the holding stress, that frees the typical knot (thick) and the easiest
        quarter (thin), against how long it lasts; grey bars: what a slow out-breath, a subtle breath and minutes of slow breathing do.</p>

      <h3>3.4 The perforator view in detail</h3>
      <p><em>Trees.</em> <Fragment set:html={text('T1', 'family')} /></p>
      <p><em>Stiffness.</em> <Fragment set:html={text('T1', 'stiff')} /></p>
      <p><em>Rolling and warmth.</em> <Fragment set:html={text('T1', 'rolled')} /> <Fragment set:html={text('T1', 'warmth')} /></p>
      <div class="two-sw">
        <figure class="sw" set:html={switchChart((byId('T1') as unknown as { switch?: Switch }).switch)} />
        <figure class="sw" set:html={switchChart((byId('T4') as unknown as { switch?: Switch }).switch)} />
      </div>
      <p class="figcap">Figure 2. Two of the switches. Left: a perforator's wall, open or shut over a band of tone. Right: the gliding
        layer, fluid or jammed over a band of movement: a jammed patch stays jammed under movement that keeps a fluid one fluid.</p>

      <h3>3.5 The reports and the models</h3>
      <p>Only after the models had been run did we set practitioners' reports against them (Table 2). The perforator model, the most
        detailed of the seven, reproduces the most; attention alone points to the latch and the motor switch; the layers' stiffness is
        shared by densification, trigger points, the motor switch and the perforators' sleeves; four reports reach no model yet.</p>
      <table class="t rep">
        <caption>Table 2. In how many of the settings where it could happen each model's knots do what is reported (— no route).</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Report</th>{g.theories.map((th) => <th>{th.name}</th>)}</tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {(g.reports ?? []).filter((r) => r.id !== 'stress').map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{r.label}</th>
              {ids.map((id) => <td>{rep(r.id, id)}</td>)}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>4. Discussion</h2>
      <p><strong>More than one kind of knot.</strong> No single model reproduces every report. A perforator held shut, with its sleeve,
        accounts for the spark, the release with a roller or a lifting hand, stiffness that outlasts the release until the layer is
        moved, and larger knots freeing smaller ones; heat, once the breaths' own share is taken out, frees about as many latch knots
        as perforator knots. It does not account for knots that let go to attention alone, which a
        latched clamp or a latched motor unit would. The simplest reading is that practitioners meet at least two kinds of knot, and a
        recording should classify each release rather than crown one theory.</p>
      <p><strong>Perforators and latches: one muscle, two holds.</strong> Both are vascular smooth muscle held contracted, and heat
        reaches both through the same number in these models. They differ in what holds them: a perforator by its wall's mechanics (a
        band of tone over which open and shut are both stable), the latch by information (a prediction that keeps its vessels clamped
        and the region out of awareness). They need not compete: a held prediction could be the command that keeps a perforator inside
        its band. Such a knot would let go to attention and to a roller alike, and spark as it went: the combination is the next model
        to build.</p>
      <p><strong>Peeling and filling.</strong> Some describe the fascia beneath the skin peeling apart along lines, leaving places that
        feel hollow, and then a filling-in felt as re-stitching. The perforator view suggests where to look: the perforators and their
        sleeves are the stitches that tether the superficial fascia to the deep; if many sleeves free at once and the returning flood
        fills the gliding plane, the layers could part along the lines between tethers, and close again as the fluid is taken up and the
        sleeves re-hold. This is not modelled; the fascia as a system of its own is the next model to build.</p>
      <p><strong>What would refute each.</strong> A structural line in a model, one that holds in every setting, is what a measurement can
        refute. A perforator knot that lets go while a hand still presses it; a latch knot that lets go with no rise in flow; a trigger point
        with no change in the muscle's stiffness; a densified patch whose layers slide as before; a nerve's knot a local anaesthetic cannot
        touch; a motor-switch knot with no unit falling silent; and, for perception, any change in the tissue that times itself to the felt
        release.</p>
      <p><strong>Limits.</strong> Most numbers specific to knots are guesses, marked and sampled; the models' traits say which of them
        decide each result. Three of the seven formalisations (the latch, densification, nerves) are ours, not yet checked by those who hold
        them. The shared scale gives every theory knots to study, by construction. The reports are rough impressions. A model shows what a
        mechanism would do with these numbers; it cannot show that knots are that mechanism.</p>
      <p><strong>Safety.</strong> Deep somatic and contemplative work can cause severe and lasting difficulties
        {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('lindahl2017', 'farias2020')} />. Nothing here is a practice or a protocol for anyone to try; any
        recording belongs under ethics review, with ordinary breathing, comfortable pressure, and nothing on the front of the neck.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>5. The measurement</h2>
      <p>A pilot of twelve participants with three to five knots each, about ninety minutes, gives forty to sixty release events, each
        marked by the participant with a button. At each: laser speckle over the knot and a pressed sham
        {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('roustit2010', 'minson2001')} />; high-density surface EMG over the upper trapezius, decomposed into
        single motor units {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('negro2016', 'dideriksen2016')} />; and ultrasound of the layers' sliding
        {' '}<Fragment set:html={cite('langevin2011')} />, before, at, and after the release and after the shoulder moves; with a blinded
        Doppler map of perforators under the knots. The predictions are fixed in advance by the models' runs. The protocol is on one
        page: <a href={`${base}/research/pilot/`}>the pilot</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="small">
      <h2>Data, code and assistance</h2>
      <p>All models, parameters with their sources, runs and figures are open at {repo}, and every figure and number on this page is
        generated from them (field guide run {g.run.inputs}). The models, the code and this text were developed with an AI assistant
        (Claude, Anthropic); the author is responsible for the content, and errors are welcome as issues.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="refs">
      <h2>References</h2>
      <ol>
        {order.map((id) => {
          const r = refById(id);
          return r ? (
            <li id={`ref-${id}`}>
              {r.authors}{r.year > 0 ? ` (${r.year})` : ''}. {r.title}. <em>{r.venue}</em>.{' '}
              {r.url && <a href={r.url}>{r.url.replace('https://', '')}</a>}
            </li>
          ) : (
            <li id={`ref-${id}`}>{id === 'knotsrepo' ? `Knots of Existence: models, parameters and runs. ${repo}` : id}</li>
          );
        })}
      </ol>
    </section>
  </div>
</Doc>

